Hand the old fork length to the truncate shim.

The shim deletes the block objects past the new end, and it finds them
by listing the fork's whole prefix. Postgres already knows where the
fork ended: smgrtruncate takes old_nblocks and passes it to every
smgr_truncate, and this one has been dropping it on the floor.

That list is not free. In redo it is one per fork per Storage TRUNCATE
record, three for a heap, each waiting for the last, inside the startup
process before the cluster can accept a connection. On a long fork it
is a page of results per thousand keys on top of that.

With the length across, a short tail names the objects it is dropping
and asks the store nothing, and a truncate that asks for a fork longer
than the one that is there returns without touching anything, which is
the early exit mdtruncate takes for the same reason: a replica
restarting from a restartpoint below a truncate replays it against a
fork that is already the shorter length.

InvalidBlockNumber goes across as is and reads as unknown on the other
side, which falls back to the list.

See zou #382.

--- a/src/backend/storage/smgr/zou_smgr.c
+++ b/src/backend/storage/smgr/zou_smgr.c
@@ -63,7 +63,7 @@
 extern int	zou_smgr_put_size(uint32 spc, uint32 db, uint32 rel, uint32 fork,
 							  uint32 nblocks);
 extern int	zou_smgr_truncate(uint32 spc, uint32 db, uint32 rel, uint32 fork,
-							  uint32 nblocks);
+							  uint32 old_blocks, uint32 nblocks);
 extern int	zou_smgr_unlink(uint32 spc, uint32 db, uint32 rel, uint32 fork);
 extern int	zou_pageserve_on(void);
 
@@ -796,10 +796,17 @@
 zoutruncate(SMgrRelation reln, ForkNumber forknum, BlockNumber old_blocks,
 			BlockNumber nblocks)
 {
-	/* against a checkpoint's flush putting the old length back */
+	/*
+	 * The old length goes across too. Without it the shim has to LIST
+	 * the fork's whole prefix to find the blocks past the new end, and
+	 * in redo that is one list per fork per Storage TRUNCATE record,
+	 * serialized in the startup process. With it a small truncate names
+	 * the objects it is dropping and asks the store nothing (zou #382).
+	 * InvalidBlockNumber is passed through as is and reads as unknown.
+	 */
 	zou_relsize_flush_block();
 	zou_check(zou_smgr_truncate(ZOU_LOCATOR(reln), (uint32) forknum,
-								(uint32) nblocks),
+								(uint32) old_blocks, (uint32) nblocks),
 			  "truncate", reln, forknum);
 	zou_relsize_note(reln->smgr_rlocator.locator, forknum,
 					 nblocks, ZOU_RELSIZE_SET);
